      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Isolated distal deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is not traditionally viewed as a potentially life-threatening condition. There are conflicting recommendations regarding its evaluation and treatment, and wide variability in clinical practice. The presentation of this case highlights the fatal potential of this condition."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "CASE REPORT",
            "content": "This is the report of a previously healthy young woman who presented to the emergency department with calf pain concerning for a DVT. She received two radiologist-performed duplex ultrasound examinations of the affected extremity, both of which were negative, but suffered a sudden cardiac arrest several hours after the second study. Autopsy attributed the death to DVT and pulmonary embolism. WHY SHOULD AN EMERGENCY PHYSICIAN BE AWARE OF THIS?: This case highlights the risk for fatal pulmonary embolization, even after normal serial ultrasound examinations to exclude DVT."
          }
        ]
      },
